From 8c24b8da9833ae16f950e7c62a882d9ade66b05f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nick <nickjb78@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Apr 2026 20:50:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] fix(input): refocus on click when keyboard focus drifted to a
 layer surface
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

processMouseDownNormal's refocus condition only triggered when the
clicked window differed from FocusState::window(), but that toplevel
window pointer is NOT updated when keyboard focus moves to a layer
surface via mouseMoveUnified's layer-focus path (rawSurfaceFocus
without rawWindowFocus). So after the user clicks on the desktop bg
layer / topbar / taskbar / notification daemon, focusState->window()
still points at the previous window, and a click back into that
previous window short-circuits the refocus call.

The keyboard surface meanwhile points at the layer client. Clients
of the layer (singularity-desktop with KeyboardMode::OnDemand, in
practice) have keyboard focus; any text-input-v3 client like
Claude Desktop / Electron apps with --enable-wayland-ime
--wayland-text-input-version=3 / browsers / Signal / VS Code that
was the focus before the layer click goes dead-typing until something
else perturbs focus state (workspace switch, Alt+Tab, mouse over a
different window).

Fix: also call refocus() when the keyboard-focus client doesn't
match the click-target window's client. refocus → mouseMoveUnified
already self-dedups when no actual change is needed, so the extra
condition is safe in the no-drift case (it's just an additional
trigger for the drift case).

Live-uprobe-verified on bare metal: click on desktop bg fires
ti_leave on Claude TI; click back on Claude now fires ti_enter on
Claude TI (pre-patch: NO events, focus stayed trapped on desktop
layer).

Stacks on top of d14e4f07 (no-IME text-input-v3 enter delivery), 
together they make typing recover automatically on the click-back
gesture without an IME being present.

Co-Authored-By: singularity <singularity@entersingularity.ai>
---
 src/managers/input/InputManager.cpp | 20 +++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/managers/input/InputManager.cpp b/src/managers/input/InputManager.cpp
index 64825633..eec65303 100644
--- a/src/managers/input/InputManager.cpp
+++ b/src/managers/input/InputManager.cpp
@@ -794,8 +794,26 @@ void CInputManager::processMouseDownNormal(const IPointer::SButtonEvent& e) {
             if (*PFOLLOWMOUSE == 3) // don't refocus on full loose
                 break;
 
+            // Singularity patch: also refocus when click hits a window
+            // whose client differs from the current keyboard-focus
+            // client. Upstream's `focusState->window() != w` is a
+            // false-negative when keyboard focus has drifted to a
+            // layer surface (clicking on the desktop bg, topbar,
+            // taskbar, notification daemon, etc.) while the toplevel
+            // "focused window" pointer in FocusState still points at
+            // the previous window. Without this extra check, clicking
+            // back into the previous window does not trigger refocus,
+            // keyboard focus stays trapped on the layer, and any
+            // text-input-v3 client (Electron, browsers) goes dead
+            // until the user moves to a different window first. Live-
+            // uprobe-verified on the singularity-desktop layer
+            // (KeyboardMode::OnDemand).
+            const auto KBSURF      = g_pSeatManager->m_state.keyboardFocus.lock();
+            const auto KBCLIENT    = KBSURF ? KBSURF->client() : nullptr;
+            const auto WCLIENT     = (w && w->wlSurface() && w->wlSurface()->resource()) ? w->wlSurface()->resource()->client() : nullptr;
+            const bool clientDrift = w && WCLIENT && KBCLIENT != WCLIENT;
             if ((g_pSeatManager->m_mouse.expired() || !isConstrained()) /* No constraints */
-                && (w && Desktop::focusState()->window() != w) /* window should change */) {
+                && (w && (Desktop::focusState()->window() != w || clientDrift)) /* window OR keyboard-focus client should change */) {
                 // a bit hacky
                 // if we only pressed one button, allow us to refocus. m_lCurrentlyHeldButtons.size() > 0 will stick the focus
                 if (m_currentlyHeldButtons.size() == 1) {
